---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: lvi-devspace-runtime-governance
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  lvi — the Elohim Protocol devspace peer-runtime (P2P-sharable development environments:
  openvscode-server, doorway-projected preview URLs, semi-ephemeral peer stewardship). A
  self-contained module designed to graduate to its own ethosengine/lvi repository — it roots
  its own governance so the discipline travels with the module across submodule extraction. lvi
  COMPOSES its siblings and must never re-implement them: content addressing → eprfs-core::BlobCid
  + elohim/epr; mount/materialization → eprfs ProjectionManifest + LocalMaterializer; build-graph
  → rakia; covenant/source-closures → brit; ingress → doorway HostRegistry. The rules below surface
  lvi's load-bearing invariants at edit-time. Full discipline: CLAUDE.md. Governing spec:
  docs/specs/2026-07-20-elohim-native-devspace-design.md.
rules:
  - id: co-resident-safety-on-sandbox-edits
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["podman", "rootless", "cgroup", "seccomp", "pids-limit", "spawn", "Command::new",
                     "ResourceQuota", "sandbox", "ActionKind::Devspace", "supervise", "reap"]
    validator: epr:validator-lvi-invariants
    why: >
      CO-RESIDENT SAFETY IS THE LOAD-BEARING INVARIANT. A devspace runs a trusted peer's UNVETTED
      code on a host that is also a live conductor/DHT participant. The sandbox MUST carry a hard
      resource quota (--memory --cpus --pids-limit, disk ceiling, --network=none by default) that
      isolates the devspace from the co-resident conductor. A fork-bomb / disk-filler / memory-hog
      must be quota-killed WITHOUT endangering the host's protocol participation — this is the floor
      the containment scenario proves. And do not conflate the layers: the trust graph ADMITS (reach
      + attestation + delegates-compute), the sandbox CONTAINS, the TTL BOUNDS. Revocation is
      lease-expiry, NOT interrupt — never design it as SIGKILL-on-DHT-signal.
  - id: mount-dont-ship-on-materialization-edits
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["LocalMaterializer", "ProjectionManifest", "MaterializationPolicy", "FetchMissing",
                     "materialize", "image", "layer", "tarball", "docker", "OCI"]
    validator: epr:validator-lvi-invariants
    why: >
      MOUNT, DON'T SHIP. The image dies as a unit. Consume eprfs ProjectionManifest +
      LocalMaterializer (Sparse / FetchMissing) — do NOT re-implement image/layer/pull/tarball
      machinery. The closure converges onto disk exactly as far as execution demands (hydrate on
      touch, verify-by-content-hash before it lands, discard on teardown). If you are writing an
      "image builder" or a "pull" path in lvi, stop: that concept is not lvi's to own.
  - id: authorization-reuse-on-grant-edits
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["delegates-compute", "delegates_compute", "bounds_validator", "Commitment",
                     "X-API-Key", "api_key", "authorize", "grant"]
    validator: epr:validator-lvi-invariants
    why: >
      AUTHORIZATION IS A Mishpat::Commitment `delegates-compute`, validated by the copy-of-
      `replicates-commons` bounds_validator (the 7 generic checks) — never a bespoke token or grant.
      "matthew stewards THIS devspace for THIS agent, bounded by THESE epr scopes, until THIS TTL"
      is a single notarized, walkable commitment that DISPLACES the X-API-Key pattern. Do not
      invent a parallel access-control primitive.
  - id: no-derived-bytes-in-commons-on-state-edits
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.rs"
      contains-any: ["persistent", "checkpoint", "cache", "target_dir", "node_modules", "sccache",
                     "NodeSeed", "seal", "custody"]
    validator: epr:validator-lvi-invariants
    why: >
      DERIVED BYTES NEVER ENTER COMMONS CUSTODY (the discipline the wider project corrected 3x).
      Source edits seal to a brit NodeSeed (authority-free checkpoint that replays on rematerialize).
      Build caches (target/, node_modules, sccache) are a steward-affinity LEASE or re-derived —
      never commons blobs. Stateful-service data is REFUSE-BY-DEFAULT unless an explicit `persistent`
      set is declared in the seed AND verified on teardown; the author-declared-but-unverified path
      is the silent-data-loss mode and does not ship.
validators:
  - ref: epr:validator-lvi-invariants
    fuel: 200
---

# lvi/ — devspace peer-runtime (self-contained governance region)

lvi is a `covers: subtree` governance region: it claims and governs its own subtree, and it carries
its full governance *inside* the module (`.epr-meta` + `CLAUDE.md` + `docs/`) so that when it
graduates to its own `ethosengine/lvi` repository the `.git` boundary makes it self-governing
automatically — the same way the vendored submodules (`brit`, `rakia`) do. It deliberately does
**not** set `root: true`: while in-tree, the `elohim/.epr-meta` interface-first-reuse gate must keep
cascading over lvi (lvi consumes the canonical CID/codec interfaces and must never re-implement
them). This manifest ADDS the four lvi-specific invariants on top of that inherited gate.

## The four invariants (reminders, not gates)

The rules above are **inject advisories** — they teach, they never block. Each fires only when an
edit touches the code surface it guards (a `contains-any` smell), so ordinary edits are untouched.
`epr:validator-lvi-invariants` is intentionally **not registered** in v1 (`REFERENCE_VALIDATORS`),
so every rule degrades to an inject nudge that surfaces the invariant and its rationale. When a real
semantic validator-EPR is wired under this name, the rules graduate to live checks with no manifest
edit — the `why:` already carries the destination.

Unlike the `bridges/did` conformance rules (which exist for drifts that happened at birth), lvi's
invariants are **design-known sharp edges** — they come from the adversarial verdicts in the
governing spec (co-resident containment; mount-don't-ship; authorization reuse; no-derived-bytes-
in-commons), planted proactively before the first line of the actuator is written. The intent is
that the runtime is *born* honoring them.

| Invariant | Guarded surface | Canonical home it routes to |
|---|---|---|
| Co-resident safety (hard quota; admit≠contain≠bound; lease-not-interrupt) | sandbox / supervision / reap code | `lvi-actuator`; the containment a2o scenario |
| Mount, don't ship | materialization code | eprfs `LocalMaterializer` + `ProjectionManifest` |
| Authorization reuse | grant/auth code | `delegates-compute` + copy-of-`replicates-commons` `bounds_validator` |
| No derived bytes in commons | state/cache/checkpoint code | brit `NodeSeed` seal; steward-affinity lease |

Full narrative discipline and the dependency-direction boundary live in `CLAUDE.md`; the dated
design and the two-slice scope live in `docs/specs/2026-07-20-elohim-native-devspace-design.md`.
